cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, dataset, or paper, please cite as below."
title: "ConfabQA: Hidden-State Refusal and Correctness Signals in Three Small Language Models"
abstract: >-
  When a small language model gives a confidently wrong answer (a confabulation),
  does its hidden state at the moment of commitment already encode the warning sign?
  This paper introduces ConfabQA, a 784-item probing benchmark with a 4x3
  (domain x category) design that breaks the cutoff/correctness confound, and
  applies it across Qwen3-1.7B, Gemma 2 2B, and Llama 3.2 3B with a multi-model
  multi-dataset bootstrap, refusal-channel attribution, and sparse-autoencoder
  decomposition. Findings: the hidden-state probe gap over the prompt-text
  baseline is sharply model-dependent (Llama 3.2 3B adds +21 to +25 pp where
  Qwen3-1.7B adds +4 to +10 pp on the same data); a within-family scaling
  control rules out parameter count as the dominant driver; the bulk of
  Llama's lead is genuine correctness self-knowledge, not refusal-channel
  readout; and SAE feature 2191 in Qwen3-1.7B alone is causally sufficient
  to flip wrong-item next-token argmaxes to refusal openers.
